WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.463 --> 00:00:06.673
Iconic!

00:00:17.809 --> 00:00:20.478
I love to know the life of the stuff I'm going to eat

00:00:20.478 --> 00:00:22.689
before it shows up on my plate.

00:00:24.065 --> 00:00:27.277
That's why I'm in Canada's
Maritime Provinces and Quebec

00:00:27.277 --> 00:00:29.446
during the season of bounty.

00:00:29.529 --> 00:00:32.073
To connect with the land and rich history

00:00:32.073 --> 00:00:33.950
through people and food.

00:00:37.704 --> 00:00:38.288
Good morning!

00:00:38.288 --> 00:00:39.581
Hi, I’m Matthieu.

00:00:42.876 --> 00:00:45.128
It's a region famous for its Gaelic,

00:00:45.128 --> 00:00:47.297
Mi'kmaq and Acadian cultures.

00:00:49.716 --> 00:00:52.010
And a bounty of heritage farm animals.

00:00:53.303 --> 00:00:57.182
They are turning this place that 
used to be a monoculture of corn

00:00:57.182 --> 00:00:58.892
into a place of biodiversity,

00:00:58.892 --> 00:01:02.437
bringing tons of animals and trying 
to feed the ground and create

00:01:02.437 --> 00:01:05.482
this virtuous cycle
that will bring more life.

00:01:06.608 --> 00:01:07.859
The skin is so thin...

00:01:09.778 --> 00:01:10.820
It's like caviar.

00:01:11.154 --> 00:01:14.657
The idea is that with so much 
biodiversity that we have going on,

00:01:15.658 --> 00:01:17.494
it'll naturally take care of itself.

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:26.044
With the longest coastline in the world,

00:01:26.044 --> 00:01:30.131
Canada's communities in Nova Scotia 
and New Brunswick are dependent

00:01:30.173 --> 00:01:34.803
on lobster fishing, which makes up about
half of their fisheries industry.

00:01:35.095 --> 00:01:37.639
Making it a crucial source of revenue

00:01:38.264 --> 00:01:39.682
and cultural pride.

00:01:53.238 --> 00:01:56.366
Cuz down here in Cap Pelé, we got the best
tasting lobster in the world.

00:01:56.658 --> 00:01:57.283
Is that so?

00:01:57.283 --> 00:01:57.742
Yep.

00:01:58.159 --> 00:02:01.955
There’s 250 traps. We lift up all the traps every day.

00:02:01.955 --> 00:02:05.333
Put fresh bait. Put that back in the water.

00:02:05.708 --> 00:02:09.379
So you need to measure every lobster
to make sure it's a legal one.

00:02:09.671 --> 00:02:13.299
Down here it’s 79 millimeters and up.

00:02:13.299 --> 00:02:16.803
The females with eggs
you have to throw them back.

00:02:17.804 --> 00:02:18.888
A good lobster fisherman?

00:02:18.888 --> 00:02:20.265
Yeah, what makes a good lobster fisherman?

00:02:20.265 --> 00:02:22.976
You need to have it right here...
You need to have love.

00:02:22.976 --> 00:02:24.936
You need to be passionate
about what you do.

00:02:24.936 --> 00:02:27.939
And you need to go out
every day and do your best.

00:02:29.399 --> 00:02:32.861
I always love stories that deal
with people that are on the edge

00:02:32.861 --> 00:02:37.240
of fitting in fully but also very proud 
of where they are standing.

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:44.038
Visiting the ancestral homelands

00:02:44.038 --> 00:02:46.916
of the Mi’kmaq people 
along the Miramichi River

00:02:48.626 --> 00:02:53.173
is an opportunity to experience
the growing indigenous tourism industry

00:02:53.381 --> 00:02:56.593
and connect with the land and history
of the region through the people

00:02:56.593 --> 00:02:58.887
who have called it home for centuries.

00:03:00.221 --> 00:03:02.682
We got really, really high water
today.

00:03:03.141 --> 00:03:03.766
Yep.

00:03:05.351 --> 00:03:06.269
Because of all the rain?

00:03:06.519 --> 00:03:07.020
Yeah.

00:03:07.228 --> 00:03:08.605
Throw it out. Let it fall.

00:03:08.605 --> 00:03:09.731
Let it fall?

00:03:09.731 --> 00:03:10.940
And then let it drift.

00:03:10.940 --> 00:03:12.859
You take your thumb and your hand

00:03:12.859 --> 00:03:14.694
and you're just going to 
bring the rod up to here.

00:03:15.236 --> 00:03:18.156
And then wait a split
second and then throw it out.

00:03:21.784 --> 00:03:22.327
It's good?

00:03:22.785 --> 00:03:24.454
Yeah. It's your first cast.

00:03:24.454 --> 00:03:25.705
Okay, excellent!

00:03:25.705 --> 00:03:27.415
And then your heart starts pounding.

00:03:28.541 --> 00:03:29.709
Boom, boom, boom, boom, boom.

00:03:32.128 --> 00:03:34.547
This is wild mint
and we use it as medicine.

00:03:35.715 --> 00:03:38.885
Smell that? Isn't that beautiful?

00:03:41.012 --> 00:03:44.182
These are tea berries
and they're very bitter.

00:03:44.265 --> 00:03:45.600
A little bit, yeah, towards the end.

00:03:48.102 --> 00:03:49.896
We want to have good food.

00:03:49.896 --> 00:03:52.398
We want to have clean water, clean air.

00:03:52.398 --> 00:03:54.442
Pass things on to our kids.

00:03:54.442 --> 00:03:58.446
And for a long time, our culture 
was denied that opportunity.

00:03:58.571 --> 00:04:02.867
We can finally stand up for ourselves
and we can be proud of who we are.

00:04:03.159 --> 00:04:07.705
Proud of just living and surviving as Mi’kmaq 
people here on the East Coast of Canada.

00:04:11.125 --> 00:04:11.584
Stay there.

00:04:12.502 --> 00:04:15.129
Mama mia, look at these beauties!

00:04:17.131 --> 00:04:18.258
How are the mustard pickles?

00:04:18.508 --> 00:04:19.259
Very good.

00:04:21.261 --> 00:04:22.303
Legend has it

00:04:22.303 --> 00:04:25.306
that the Hopewell rocks
formed from the pots

00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:28.851
Mi’kmaq people once used 
to feast and celebrate with.

00:04:29.394 --> 00:04:33.147
Walking on the seafloor
of the Bay of Fundy, due to its famous

00:04:33.147 --> 00:04:37.235
extreme variability in tides, it's
easy to feel the history

00:04:37.485 --> 00:04:39.404
and significance of this land.

00:04:44.117 --> 00:04:46.369
A quick flight and we are in Quebec.

00:04:47.078 --> 00:04:49.914
Taking its name from the 
famous Mont Royal Park

00:04:49.914 --> 00:04:54.669
which overlooks the city, Montréal
is one of the greenest cities in the world

00:04:54.752 --> 00:04:59.007
for its sustainability
and its many green spaces.

00:04:59.799 --> 00:05:02.468
On top of the city's convention center

00:05:02.468 --> 00:05:04.262
sits an unexpected farm.

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:08.808
Montréal is really the world
capital of urban agriculture.

00:05:09.267 --> 00:05:13.896
There’s almost 60 urban farms, it’s
double the amount of similar cities.

00:05:14.397 --> 00:05:16.065
I want to transform the city.

00:05:16.065 --> 00:05:20.570
Farm on the rooftop will have an impact
for biodiversity in downtown.

00:05:21.029 --> 00:05:21.446
Chard.

00:05:21.446 --> 00:05:22.280
Rainbow chard.

00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:22.530
Yeah.

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:23.031
Tomatoes.

00:05:23.906 --> 00:05:24.657
Oh, my God.

00:05:33.791 --> 00:05:36.169
When I'm doing a dish,

00:05:36.252 --> 00:05:40.840
I'm looking at like,
what's the season vegetables.

00:05:42.759 --> 00:05:47.138
The restaurants in Montréal, they want to
work with farms to stay local.

00:05:47.138 --> 00:05:49.057
You're going to get a live reaction here.

00:05:49.057 --> 00:05:50.475
Are you afraid, are you scared?

00:05:50.475 --> 00:05:51.726
Yes, I'm stressed.

00:05:56.439 --> 00:05:57.565
It's great that she's

00:05:57.565 --> 00:06:00.151
focusing on seasonality in the dishes.

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:03.279
Meeting people

00:06:03.279 --> 00:06:07.450
and having meaningful encounters
is something that really stays with me.

00:06:07.533 --> 00:06:11.245
Canadians are defined as being, quote
unquote, nice.

00:06:11.287 --> 00:06:13.539
I think they have much
more character than that.

00:06:13.539 --> 00:06:17.919
There is a sense of contemplation
and excitement to see that you can nurture

00:06:17.919 --> 00:06:21.339
your soul, nurture yourself, 
nurture other people,

00:06:21.422 --> 00:06:24.634
and a love for their environment 
that I really enjoyed.

